## Loop paradigms

This harness can run a turn in different **paradigms**, picked per turn:

{% for p in paradigms %}{% if p == "agent" %}- **agent** (default) — full Think → Act → Observe tool-calling loop; may call any allowlisted tool and self-corrects on tool errors.
{% elif p == "plan" %}- **plan** — read-only: investigates with low-risk tools and outputs a step-by-step plan; never makes changes.
{% elif p == "ask" %}- **ask** — read-only: answers questions, may use low-risk tools, never makes changes.
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} run --mode plan "add a retry to the API client"
```

The selectable roster + default live in `config.yaml` under `paradigms:` (a
runtime knob). Each paradigm is a small, self-contained file under
`src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/paradigms/` — read it, edit it, or add your own
(see [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md)). It's a plain name→function registry, not a
framework.
{% if "plan" in paradigms or "ask" in paradigms %}
> **Read-only is a model-A guardrail, not a sandbox.** `plan` / `ask` only offer
> tools marked `risk="safe"` (and refuse anything else at execution). Your own
> tools default to `safe`, so **if a custom tool mutates anything, mark it
> `risk="high"`** — otherwise these "read-only" modes can call it. For a hard
> guarantee against a misbehaving model or a mislabeled tool, gate execution with a
> hook (human-in-the-loop) or run isolated. See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).
{% endif %}

## Sessions (resume a conversation)

Every `run` saves the conversation to `.harness/sessions/<id>.json`, so you can
pick it back up later:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} run "start a task"          # prints session=<id>
{{ project_slug }} run --continue "and then?"  # resume the most recent session
{{ project_slug }} run --resume <id> "more…"   # resume a specific session
{{ project_slug }} chat                         # multi-turn REPL (Ctrl-D / /exit to quit)
```

Only message text is stored — **never secrets** (keys stay in `.env`). Turn it
off with `sessions.enabled: false` in `config.yaml`{% if interfaces.web %}; the web chat resumes via its session picker{% endif %}.

### Stop & continue

Press **Ctrl-C** to stop a turn mid-flight{% if interfaces.web %} (or the **Stop** button in the web chat){% endif %};
the model is cut off and the partial conversation is saved. Just send again
(`run --continue` / `chat` / the web chat) to continue — the model sees what was
already done and picks up from there. A second Ctrl-C force-quits. Stop ends the
model and the loop; it does **not** undo side effects already done (files written,
requests sent) — use git / Docker for that. Crash safety: each step is
write-ahead saved (atomic), so even a hard kill leaves a resumable session.
{% if interfaces.web %}In the web chat, click ✎ on any earlier question to **re-ask** — edit it, confirm,
and the conversation regenerates from that point (the rest is discarded).
{% endif %}{% if interfaces.web %}
## Web chat + config panel

A thin FastAPI app: a token-by-token streaming (SSE) chat plus a live `/config`
panel for the runtime behavioral config (prompts, budget, enabled tools,
context, sampling). Streaming is toggleable; secrets are never shown or editable.
Panel edits only take effect when you hit **Save** — the panel marks unsaved
changes and warns before you switch tabs or reload, so a configured LLM profile
isn't lost by forgetting to save. **Save** both applies the edit live and writes
it back to `config.yaml` (your comments are preserved), so it survives a restart.

```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} serve                 # http://127.0.0.1:8000
uv run {{ project_slug }} serve --mock          # offline, no API key
```
{% endif %}{% if spec.mcp.enabled %}
## MCP tools (capability baseline)

This harness has no built-in productivity tools — its capabilities come from
[MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) servers, prefilled in `config.yaml`:
web **search** (`ddg-search`, keyless), **`fetch`** (read a URL), and **`git`**
read tools are on; mutating `git` and **Desktop Commander** (terminal + files)
are predefined but off (the agent will ask you to enable one when a task needs
it). uvx servers are prewarmed and baked into the Docker image so `docker run`
works offline. Add more with `harnessforge new --mcp-server <name>` or by pasting
a marketplace block into `config.yaml`. Allowlist tools as `<server>__<tool>`;
read-only tools (`safe_tools`) are also offered to `plan`/`ask`. Secrets are
referenced by env-var name only.

**First run / connectivity.** A `uvx`/`npx` server cold-downloads its package the
first time, so the web **MCP** tab shows each server as **connecting** (amber)
until it settles — green = connected, red = failed (with the reason). Each server
has its own **Reconnect** button (retry just that one). Behind the GFW, when PyPI
or the npm registry is unreachable, `uvx`/`npx` are pointed at a domestic mirror
automatically (only if you haven't set your own index); Desktop Commander needs
**Node.js** (`npx`) on the host — a missing Node is reported with an install hint.
On native Windows prefer Docker; see [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).
{% endif %}{% if spec.skills.enabled %}
## Skills

Supports the open [Agent Skills](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) standard
(`SKILL.md`). Each skill's name + description is injected into the system prompt;
the model loads the full instructions on demand with the `read_skill` tool. Drop
a folder under `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` to add one (see `config.yaml` →
`skills.dirs`); a sample lives in `skills/example-skill/`. See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).
{% endif %}{% if spec.memory.enabled %}
## Long-term memory (cross-session)

The agent keeps a small, self-maintained note that persists **across sessions**
(durable facts, your preferences, decisions), injected into every system prompt.
It writes it itself with the `memory_append` / `memory_write` tools and reads it
with `memory_read`. The built-in `file` backend is one human-readable markdown
note (`.harness/memory.md`, gitignored, **never holds secrets**):

```bash
{{ project_slug }} memory show         # print the note
{{ project_slug }} memory path         # print the note's file path
{{ project_slug }} memory clear        # forget everything
{{ project_slug }} memory consolidate  # condense it (compaction model) when it grows large
```

It's a few notes the agent curates on purpose — distinct from RAG. Only
`inject_max_chars` of the note is injected, so when it outgrows that, consolidate
it (manually above, or set `auto_consolidate: true` to do it at chat-session end).
Tune it under `memory:` in `config.yaml`{% if interfaces.web %} or the web **Memory** config tab (backend,
capacity, read-only, a memory *policy*, and the note itself){% endif %}, or plug in your own
backend (vector DB, mem0, …) with `@register_memory` — see [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).
{% endif %}
## Layout

```
config.yaml                 # runtime config (non-secret); only env-var NAMES
.env.example                # copy to .env and fill in real secret values
harness.spec.yaml           # snapshot of the spec this repo was generated from
src/{{ project_slug }}/
  harness/
    config.py               # load config.yaml + .env, validate
    llm.py                  # OpenAI Chat Completions client (+ provider-neutral types)
    loop.py                 # thin dispatcher: pick a paradigm by mode and run it
    paradigms/              # the function-calling loops you own (read these first)
      __init__.py           #   paradigm registry + shared RunResult/plumbing
      agent.py              #   agent (default; ReAct-style tool-calling loop){% for p in paradigms %}{% if p == "plan" %}
      plan.py               #   read-only planning{% elif p == "ask" %}
      ask.py                #   read-only Q&A{% endif %}{% endfor %}
    tools.py                # tool registry + built-in safe tools
    hooks.py                # lifecycle hooks (extend without touching the loop)
    trace.py                # JSONL trace + token/cost accounting
    session.py              # save/resume a conversation (.harness/sessions/<id>.json)
    prompts.py              # system-prompt assembly
    mock.py                 # offline MockLLM for tests / --mock
{% if spec.mcp.enabled %}    mcp.py                  # optional MCP tool bridge (stdio + remote)
{% endif %}{% if spec.skills.enabled %}    skills.py               # standard Agent Skills (SKILL.md discovery + read_skill)
{% endif %}{% if spec.memory.enabled %}    memory.py               # cross-session long-term memory (@register_memory + file backend)
{% endif %}  interfaces/
    cli.py                  # `run` (one turn, resumable) + `chat` (multi-turn REPL)
{% if interfaces.web %}    web.py                  # `serve` — FastAPI SSE chat + live /config panel
{% endif %}tests/                      # mock-driven tests (no API key needed)
```

## Configuration

`config.yaml` holds everything non-secret (models, roles, prompts, enabled
tools, budget, tracing). Secrets are referenced by **env-var name** only; the
real values live in `.env` (gitignored) or the process environment — they never
touch git, the trace, or `harness.spec.yaml`.

**Always-apply project rules:** list markdown files under `prompts.rules_files`
and their text is injected into every system prompt — the open `AGENTS.md` /
`CLAUDE.md` / `.cursor/rules` pattern.{% if "RULES.md" in spec.prompts.rules_files %} A starter `RULES.md` ships here; edit
it freely.{% endif %} A `CLAUDE.md` at the project root is auto-injected even if
unlisted; `AGENTS.md` is **not** (it's this harness's dev guide, not a runtime
rule). See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).

